WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:04.760
Works of art can be perplexing, and sometimes,
they can knock you dead.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.660
Netflix's Velvet Buzzsaw takes that idea and
runs with it, giving life to pieces of art

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.860
that might take your breath away permanently.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:16.320
If you're having trouble figuring out what
the film is trying to say, worry no more as

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.130
we break down the bloody end of Velvet Buzzsaw.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.560
Don't be fooled by the glossy sheen.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:26.710
Despite a high art atmosphere, a generous
budget, and a cast stacked with A-listers,

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:31.439
at its heart, Velvet Buzzsaw is as trashy
a horror movie as any installment of Friday

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:32.000
the 13th.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:37.250
But as far as plot goes, the movie can more
accurately be described as an art world version

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:38.620
of Final Destination.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:44.620
Instead of the victims being generally unsympathetic,
cookie-cutter teens, they're uniquely loathsome,

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:46.340
insufferably pretentious grown adults.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:50.640
You're flat-out not supposed to like them,
which makes it all the more exciting when

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.180
the time comes to sit back and watch them
suffer.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.700
Much like Final Destination, this is essentially
a slasher movie with a metaphysical villain.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.400
In the Final Destination series, the enemy
is Death itself.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.780
In Velvet Buzzsaw, things are a little bit
more complicated.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.680
Here, the killer is the vengeful spirit of
a deceased artist, persisting from beyond

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:15.120
the grave to lethally punish those who would
seek to profit off of his and others' art.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.880
If it sounds a little silly, that's because
it's supposed to be.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.460
Velvet Buzzsaw is not a particularly scary

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:22.960
movie.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:27.520
As far as genre goes, it's best to describe
it as a horror-comedy, with the emphasis on

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:28.020
comedy.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.460
But it's not a very funny movie either, as
far as knee-slapping jokes are concerned.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.560
Above all else, the movie is a satire about
bad people.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:41.060
Its humor comes from the absurdity of the
characters and their ridiculous world, which

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:42.740
they all take quite seriously.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.980
Think about the characters' names, which take
pretentiousness to the point of parody.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.500
There's Morf Vandewalt, Rhodora Haze, Jon
Dondon, and of course, the vengeful artist

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.460
Vetril Dease.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:59.060
All of these people are insufferable at best
and evil at worst, generally only caring about

00:01:59.060 --> 00:01:59.620
themselves.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:04.240
With characters like this, the movie's world
is already a total horror show, making the

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:05.700
killings extra cathartic.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.880
A key to understanding the movie is realizing
that it's not really trying to frighten you.

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:14.680
When these characters get what's coming to
them, you're supposed to be amused.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.620
Some of the first lines of dialogue in Velvet

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:22.510
Buzzsaw come from a mechanized artwork called
Hoboman, a mixed-media piece on display at

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:23.180
Art Basel.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:27.580
Jake Gyllenhaal's critic character, Morf,
has arrived to size up the piece, as well

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.780
as whatever else catches his eye at the show.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.760
“Have you ever felt invisible?”

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:39.780
The question appears to be commenting on homeless
people, but it also applies to Velvet Buzzsaw's

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:44.460
conception of the artist, as well as the bitter
grudge that drives the movie's story along.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:50.190
For a movie about the art world, very few
artists actually appear — most of the characters

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:53.660
are at these shows to buy, sell, hype up,
or reject art.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.950
The artists themselves are spoken of like
brand names, with their work being praised

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:01.240
for its popularity and monetary value above
all else.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:05.720
They're not treated like people who put their
hearts and souls into their creations — especially

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:07.730
the artist at the center of the plot.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.500
Morf determines the value of art by filtering

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.240
it all through his own perspective.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.020
What he doesn't like, doesn't sell.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:20.320
He may not be a gallery owner or an outright
sellout like Rhodora, but he's still a gatekeeper,

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.900
and a crucial part of the art world ecosystem.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:27.680
When Morf walks into the Art Basel show at
the start of the movie, he passes by a sign

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.400
that reads "There's no confusion in my house"
a statement that reflects his status as a

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:32.900
critic.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.840
Confusion only exists in Morf's personal life.

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:38.820
When he's at work, he speaks with exacting
certainty.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:40.980
Morf is there to pass judgment.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.600
In his world, it's up to him to determine
what qualifies as quality art.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.740
He dismisses the hit of the show, Hoboman,
as a derivative work that doesn't impress

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:50.240
him.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:54.819
His assessment effectively cancels out all
the other praise, leading the piece to languish

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:58.300
in storage after his bad review snuffs out
all interest in it.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.000
"Your review of Hoboman killed the sale.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:04.340
Instead of being seen, it's gathering dust
at a storage facility."

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.560
"I am not your mouthpiece."

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:11.820
Morf's critiques have direct consequences,
with some bad reviews apparently being so

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.140
distressing that they basically threaten artists'
lives.

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:20.280
In one instance, Morf is told about an artist
who tailspins into self-destruction after

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:23.650
being emotionally devastated by one of his
brutal reviews.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.520
"Ricky got drunk and crashed his car last
night.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:28.040
He’s in a coma.”

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:29.479
“I heard he was crushed."

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:31.000
“By the car?"

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:32.700
"Your review."

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:38.600
In the world of Velvet Buzzsaw, Morf's critiques
are presented as a kind of violence, justifying

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.900
his own eventual death at the hands of the
living Hoboman.

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:44.759
Velvet Buzzsaw positions itself as a critique

00:04:44.759 --> 00:04:49.210
of the commercialization of art, with the
vengeful spirit of Vetril Dease serving as

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:50.680
the message's main vessel.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:55.400
But it's important to remember that Dease
himself was not a particularly good person.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:00.660
As Morf and the other characters eventually
discover, Dease was very violent, and emotionally

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:01.160
disturbed.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:06.000
Death didn't turn him into a killer spirit
it really just let his dark soul loose on

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:06.540
the world.

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:11.039
Despite all of this, it seems that everything
would have been fine if Josephina had just

00:05:11.039 --> 00:05:14.490
followed Dease's wishes, and allowed his work
to be destroyed.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:17.300
Usually, blaming the victim is a nasty thing.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.719
In the context of Velvet Buzzsaw, in which
the victims are pretty much always operating

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.800
out of their own self-interest, it feels pretty
justified.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:29.500
The killer is a bad person, sure — but there
are no innocent victims in this movie.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:32.271
As haunting as Dease's paintings appear, it's

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:35.480
not necessarily his artwork that's carrying
a curse.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:39.939
Sure, his actual blood may be all mixed up
with the paint he used, but that's more a

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:43.380
signpost for the movie's themes than a key
to its twisted magic.

00:05:44.009 --> 00:05:48.900
The actual function of Dease's "curse" is
to weaponize all art, not just his own paintings.

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:53.960
This is why Hoboman comes to life to attack
Morf, why Sphere is able to cut Gretchen's

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:58.949
arm off in the movie's best kill, and why
Josephina gets consumed by a roomful of living

00:05:58.949 --> 00:05:59.440
paint.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:04.300
The movie doesn't explain its rules very well,
but the unifying thread of all the deaths

00:06:04.309 --> 00:06:08.439
is that they're committed by works of art,
against people who see art as little more

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:10.280
than something to be bought and sold.

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:14.400
With these characters being so steeped in
the art world, they're always vulnerable.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:18.440
They don't appreciate the power that surrounds
them, and end up paying for their ignorance

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:19.460
with their lives.

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:24.520
After a few kills have been tallied up, this
message becomes really, really obvious.

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:28.080
But the killings aren’t as simple as “’selling
art’ equals ‘you die.’”

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:31.340
They’re more of an indictment of people’s
whole philosophies.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:36.800
The plunder of Dease's paintings results in
multiple deaths; Bryson, the gallery installer

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:42.050
who steals some of the artwork; Jon, the gallery
owner, whose body Coco finds; Gretchen, whose

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:47.490
body Coco finds; Josephina, whose greedy desire
to raise her status as an exhibitor led her

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:52.429
to steal Dease's art in the first place; and
Morf, the destructive critic, whose body Coco

00:06:52.429 --> 00:06:53.260
also finds.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:54.560
Poor Coco.

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:58.040
“AAAH!

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:00.020
F--- me!”

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.680
Morf spends most of the movie getting increasingly

00:07:02.689 --> 00:07:06.770
suspicious about the many deaths happening
around him, but the only character to figure

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:08.600
out what's going on is Rhodora.

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:14.180
After Bryson, Jon, Gretchen, Josephina, and
Morf all die, Rhodora accepts that the insane

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:18.880
scenario of "Dease bringing art to life to
kill people" probably has some merit to it.

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:24.240
Sensing that she's probably next on the chopping
block, Rhodora hires a team of movers to strip

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:30.520
her home of all artwork — every image, every
drawing, every postcard, counting up to 47

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:31.200
pieces in total.

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:36.140
Satisfied that the job is done, she starts
to rest more easily — until she feels a

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:38.000
little tickle on the back of her neck.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:43.619
The "Velvet Buzzsaw" tattoo — a band logo
from Rhodora's days as a punk musician — is

00:07:43.619 --> 00:07:48.110
one piece of art she couldn't get rid of,
leaving her helpless as it grinds to life

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:48.900
to kill her dead.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:53.740
At the beginning of the movie, Rhodora's old
band is implied by the artist Damrish to have

00:07:53.749 --> 00:07:59.020
started from a sincere place before losing
the plot, presumably becoming more of a brand

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:02.520
name and a way of making money than a purely
artistic outlet.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:05.000
Basically, she's a sellout, and she knows
it.

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:09.280
"The early stuff was great, then it became
what, like self-parody?"

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:14.220
Damrish, being only six months removed from
a life of poverty, is still in tune with his

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:19.550
creative roots, and acutely sensitive to the
idea of selling out — a path Rhodora committed

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:21.900
to long ago for the sake of getting rich.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:26.599
It's a decision that ultimately dooms her,
guiding her directly, over the years, to a

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:27.849
most unexpected death.

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:30.520
There's a moment at the beginning of Velvet

00:08:30.529 --> 00:08:35.099
Buzzsaw in which John Malkovich's character,
an artist named Piers, expresses a desire

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:39.840
to get away from the tiresome circus of art
as business, and return to something pure.

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:44.820
When we meet him at Art Basel, he's successful,
sober, and creatively unsatisfied.

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:49.920
He seems to be unhappy, and his state of mind
is a key thing to keep in mind as you unpack

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:50.920
the movie's themes.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:57.820
"I am fighting to get back to creation, to
revelation, the billion years of energy sparking

00:08:57.820 --> 00:08:58.620
through our brain."

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:02.220
As the movie eventually concludes, this path
is a righteous one.

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:07.140
Writer-director Dan Gilroy talked about the
themes Velvet Buzzsaw explores in an interview

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:11.220
with The Hollywood Reporter, saying,
"I was very interested in the idea of the

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:14.360
relationship between art and commerce in today's
world.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:16.620
It's a very uneasy relationship.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:21.600
[…] That's not to say that commercial success
diminishes a work, but it doesn't define it,

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:22.100
either.

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:25.560
I wanted to get at the idea that art is more
than a commodity."

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:31.120
Ultimately, the movie is about elevating the
pure act of creation over crass commercialization.

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:36.680
That's why the man on the street at the movie's
end — a real hoboman — isn't in any danger

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:40.020
from selling the Dease art he finds following
Rhodora's death.

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:45.320
Sold for cheap on the street, the art is judged
on pure aesthetics, not by perceived value.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:50.340
Dease wanted his work destroyed, but at the
end of the movie, it's out in the world anonymously,

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:52.500
which also seems to be an acceptable fate.

00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:57.460
His body of work is no longer a eight-figure
batch of commodities, but once again pieces

00:09:57.460 --> 00:09:58.940
of art for art's sake.

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:02.160
Velvet Buzzsaw expresses its themes not just

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:05.080
through the people it kills, but also by the
people it doesn't.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:10.020
Poor Coco, who stumbles across so many of
the movie's dead bodies, never gets a higher-level

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:11.580
job that she so covets.

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:16.020
Because of the distance she maintains from
the art industry's rotten moral core, she

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:17.260
survives Dease's wrath.

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:22.620
Damrish, haunted by gazing into a Dease painting
and feeling as though it's watching him, just

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:26.740
gets up and leaves the movie at one point,
sensing the danger and running for his life.

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:32.280
As for Piers, he goes hermit and starts drinking
again, surviving out of his desire to get

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:35.220
away from the alienating commercial side of
the art business.

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:39.840
It's an amusing message for the movie to commit
to, but it really does go there — going

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:44.660
back to being a drunk restores Piers' creativity,
and basically saves his life.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:47.500
"I think sober hasn't been good for him."

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:52.200
"Well this was done fifteen years ago - Piers
was in the full bloom of alcoholism here."

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:52.800
"Exactly."

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:57.320
In letting these three characters survive,
the movie is saying that they're all on the

00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:58.140
correct paths.

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:02.780
In the final shot of the movie, beneath the
credits, Piers walks on a beach, scratching

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:04.560
shapes in the sand with a stick.

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:09.440
In close-up, it seems as though there could
be some bigger message in what he's doing.

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:14.280
But as the camera pans back, it becomes clear
that there is no deeper meaning to his drawings.

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:16.220
He's creating just for fun.

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:20.420
His colleagues made art into something to
consume, and so got consumed by it.

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:24.780
Piers goes back to basics, seeking that primal
creative energy.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:29.780
He ends the movie looking drunk and joyful
with his life intact, performing for no one

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:32.680
in particular, drawing aimless circles on
the beach.

